      "id": 1,
      "text": "New Holland began as Flemingsburgh on September 2, 1818, named for John Fleming who settled nearby in 1802.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Holland%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the Civil War, a segment of General Sherman's Union Army marched through New Holland’s covered bridge on the Zanesville‑Cincinnati Trace.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Holland%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "On September 18, 1899, two trains collided head‑on at the North Main Street crossing, creating a dramatic moment in town history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Holland%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "New Holland sits in both Fayette and Pickaway counties, a unique feature for a village of its size.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
